<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
                       "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Hardened Runtime exemptions required by Electron's V8 engine.
       allow-jit: V8 JIT-compiles JS into RWX pages.
       allow-unsigned-executable-memory: Electron mmaps non-MAP_JIT executable
       pages; required on every Electron app to date. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- disable-library-validation: needed because of `sharp`, NOT because of
       `better-sqlite3`. PwrAgnt also ships better-sqlite3 and does NOT need
       this entitlement — electron-builder rebuilds and re-signs the
       better-sqlite3 .node file with our Developer ID during packaging, so
       library validation is satisfied for the SQLite path.

       Sharp is two-layer: the JS↔C++ bridge `sharp.node` (which DOES get
       re-signed by electron-builder), plus a libvips-cpp.42.x.dylib that
       sharp.node dlopens at runtime. The libvips dylib lives under
       node_modules/@img/sharp-libvips-darwin-arm64/lib/ and ships
       pre-signed by sharp's maintainer (different team). The hardened
       runtime kills the dlopen unless we either disable library validation
       OR re-sign the libvips dylib ourselves with our Developer ID.

       TODO(harden): drop this entitlement and add an electron-builder
       afterPack hook that re-signs every .dylib under
       app.asar.unpacked/node_modules/@img/** with the release identity. The
       buildout plan §"Bundle Layout & Signing" already specifies this as
       the long-term shape; we're only deferring it for v0.0.1 velocity. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Resource access under the Hardened Runtime.

       An Info.plist usage string alone is NOT enough for a hardened,
       non-sandboxed app: the runtime denies the device before TCC is
       ever consulted, so the user never sees a prompt and the capture
       just yields silence. Both usage strings have shipped in
       electron-builder.yml's `mac.extendInfo` since the Fast Video
       Capture work (NSMicrophoneUsageDescription,
       NSCameraUsageDescription) while these two entitlements did not,
       which means microphone recording could not have worked in any
       signed release build — only in unsigned local dev, where the
       hardened runtime is not applied. That gap is why the audio work
       kept being hard to verify.

       audio-input covers BOTH consumers: the PwrSnapRecorder helper
       (AVCaptureSession, native/recorder/main.swift) and the renderer's
       getUserMedia level metering. `entitlementsInherit` points at this
       same file, so helper binaries and renderer processes inherit it.

       System audio is NOT here — it rides the Screen Recording grant
       via ScreenCaptureKit and needs no device entitlement. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
